    "lecture_num": 7,
    "lecture_name": "Introduction to AI-Powered Development (VibeCode)",
    "content": "Introduction to AI-Powered Development (VibeCode)\nLESSON MAP\nCore Question: How do AI coding tools amplify your existing FastAPI, Supabase, and prompt engineering knowledge to ship faster—without replacing your judgment?\nThe Starting Point:\nStudents have 8 weeks of FastAPI, Supabase, database design, and prompt engineering under their belt\nThey assume AI tools either write perfect code automatically or are just fancy autocomplete\nThey're curious but skeptical about whether these tools actually work for real projects\nThe Destination:\nStudents understand AI tools as amplifiers that require clear PRDs, technical context, and human judgment\nThey can execute a 9-step workflow from PRD to deployed app using Cursor, Lovable, and Claude Code\nThey recognize when to use which tool and how to debug AI-generated code using their existing knowledge\nThe Journey:\nOpening scenario: Two demos—vague Bolt.new failure vs. specific V0 success (GIGO principle)\nLandscape tour: Cursor Composer, Lovable, Claude Code, Gemini Pro 3, Bolt.new, V0 (tools as specialists)\nFirst hands-on: Write micro-PRD with ChatGPT, generate UI in Lovable\nDeep dive: Cursor with @codebase context, iterative refinement\nSecond hands-on: Clone, refine, test cycle\nWorkflow synthesis: The complete 9-step ship cycle\nThird hands-on: Export, edit, commit workflow\nReadiness check: \"Who can ship this weekend?\"\nKey Concepts Covered:\nAI tools as knowledge amplifiers vs. replacements\nGIGO principle (garbage in, garbage out)\nPRD-driven development with AI assist\nContext provisioning (@codebase, @docs, @file in Cursor)\nTool specialization (Lovable for UI, Cursor for refinement, Claude Code for architecture)\nThe handoff workflow (cloud tool → local environment → version control)\nConnections:\nBuilds on:",
