  {
    "lecture_num": 1,
    "lecture_name": "Introduction to AI Agents",
    "content": "Introduction to AI Agents\nInstructor: 100xEngineers\nSession Focus: Conceptual foundations of AI Agents, the \"High Agency\" mindset, and the transition from LLMs to Agentic Workflows.\n________________________________________\n🎯 Learning Outcomes\nBy the end of this lecture, you should be able to:\n•\tDefine what an AI Agent is beyond just a \"chatbot\" using the Augmented LLM framework.\n•\tUnderstand the 6-Step Agentic Loop: Sense, Plan, Act, Observe, Reflect, and Adapt.\n•\tDifferentiate between a standard LLM Workflow and a true Agentic Workflow.\n•\tApply the \"First Principles\" of agency to build systems that can function autonomously.\n•\tConceptualize a \"Tireless Assistant\" that fits your specific life/work needs.\n________________________________________\n🔑 Key Takeaways\n•\tAgency is about Loop & Adaptation: An agent isn't defined by the tools it has, but by its ability to sense its environment, act on it, and adapt its plan based on feedback (the Loop).\n•\tAugmented LLM: The modern AI agent is essentially an \"Augmented LLM\"—an LLM enhanced with retrieval capabilities (RAG) and tool execution (Function Calling).\n•\tThe \"Human Researcher\" Analogy: Agents should mimic how humans solve undefined problems: we don't just follow a script; we research, hit dead ends, learn new concepts, update our mental model, and try again.\n•\tContext is King: For an agent to be effective, it must \"sense\" the user's current state (e.g., learning week, difficulty level) to provide relevant help.\n________________________________________\n💡 What Was Covered (Chronological)\n1. The \"High Agency\" Activation Exercise\n•\tActivity: The lecture began with a time-bound challenge: Prepare a 2-minute pitch explaining why you deserve a spot at the Final Demo Day.\n•\tInstructor's Motive:\no\tTo shift students from a passive \"listening\" mode to an active \"doing\" mode immediately.\no\tTo simulate a high-stakes, time-constrained environment where \"High Agency\" (taking ownership and action) is required.\no\tTo demonstrate that in the real world (and in agent building), you must be able to articulate value and execute under pressure without perfect information.\no\tNote: The pitch itself wasn't the actual selection criteria; the exercise was about preparation and mindset.\n2. The Evolution of LLMs to Agents\nThe instructor traced the history of LLMs to explain why agents are the next logical step.\n•\tPhase 1 (Text In/Text Out): Basic LLMs. Good for generation but isolated.\n•\tPhase 2 (Context Extension): Larger context windows allowed for more data processing.\n•\tPhase 3 (RAG & Tools): We started connecting LLMs to external data (RAG) and actions (Tools).\n•\tPhase 4 (Agents): Combining RAG + Tools + A Loop (Reasoning & Adaptation).\n3. The \"Augmented LLM\" Concept\n•\tTheory: The instructor introduced the concept of the Augmented LLM, referencing the Anthropic \"Building Effective Agents\" article.\n•\tDefinition: An agent is not a magical new entity; it is an LLM that has been \"augmented\" with:\n1.\tKnowledge: Access to documents/databases (RAG).\n2.\tCapabilities: Access to tools (Search, Code Execution).\n3.\tController: A system prompt or loop that directs the LLM on when to use these augmentations.\n________________________________________\n⚙️ Core Components: The Agentic Loop\nThe instructor detailed a 6-step framework that defines an agent's behavior. This is the \"brain\" of the agent.\n1.\tSense: The agent must first understand the environment.\no\tExample: Detecting the user is in \"",
